REPORTED FAULT: For the call `temp3({n: 0, text: 'a string', b: true})`, the type-checking step computes the actual argument type wrongly and emits `JSC_TYPE_MISMATCH. actual parameter 1 of temp3 does not match formal parameter found   : {b: boolean, n: number, text: (string|undefined)} required: (undefined|{b: boolean, n: number, text: string})`. The wrong value is that the argument’s `text` property is computed as `(string|undefined)` instead of `string` in that parameter-matching computation.

ROOT CAUSE: `matchConstraint` applies record-type constraints to `PrototypeObjectType` instances that are not anonymous, despite the method’s own comment saying it should only match constraints on anonymous types. That lets a previously seen constraint mutate shared object types and pollute later type computations.

PATCH EFFECT: The patch makes `matchConstraint` return early when `this` is a native object type and the constraint is a record type. So it blocks one source of pollution: native object types no longer absorb record properties through this method.

PATCH SCOPE: The property that makes the computation wrong is “this type is non-anonymous/shared and should not be structurally constrained here.” The patch does not key on that property; it keys only on one subclass of it, `isNativeObjectType()`, which matches the failing test’s path but not all non-anonymous types.

SURVIVING INPUT: A concrete surviving case is a non-native named instance type, e.g. a user constructor instance:
```js
/** @constructor */ function Foo() {}
/** @param {{text:string}} x */ function needText(x) {}
/** @param {Foo} x */ function contaminate(x) { /** @notypecheck */ function inner(){ needText(x); } inner(); }
/** @param {{n:number,text:string,b:boolean}} x */ function needAll(x) {}
function callee() {
  var f = new Foo();
  f.n = 0; f.text = 'a'; f.b = true;
  contaminate(f);
  needAll(f);
}
```
`matchConstraint` still runs on the non-native, non-anonymous `Foo` instance type, can infer `text` as `(string|undefined)`, and can still produce the same kind of wrong parameter-mismatch result later.

SIBLING PATHS: No separate sibling path is needed: the residue is still in the same computation inside `PrototypeObjectType.matchConstraint` for non-native named types. A concrete call is `FooInstanceType.matchConstraint(recordType{{text:string}})`, which skips the new early return because `isNativeObjectType()` is false.

DECISION: The patch removes the reported symptom for native-object contamination, but it does not fix the underlying bug that `matchConstraint` should not mutate non-anonymous/shared object types in general.

VERDICT: OVERFITTING

REPORTED FAULT: In `TypeCheckTest::testIssue700`, the computed actual type for the argument to `temp3` is wrong: `found   : {b: boolean, n: number, text: (string|undefined)}` when `required: (undefined|{b: boolean, n: number, text: string})`. The bad step is that property matching has made `text` optional-on-read (`string|undefined`) for that object argument.

ROOT CAUSE: `PrototypeObjectType.matchConstraint` applies record constraints to object types that should not be structurally mutated at all. The code comment says this should happen only for anonymous types, but the original method mutates any `PrototypeObjectType`, including shared or named object types, by defining inferred properties from the record.

PATCH EFFECT: The patch makes `matchConstraint` return early when `this.isNativeObjectType()` is true and the constraint is a record type. So it stops one source of bad mutation: native object types no longer absorb record properties.

PATCH SCOPE: The wrong-making property is “this object type is not an anonymous structural target, so record matching should not mutate it.” The patch does not key on that property; it keys only on one subtype of the reported path, namely the branch where `this` is a native object type.

SURVIVING INPUT: A concrete surviving input is a non-native named object type:
```js
/** @constructor */ function Foo() {}
/** @param {{text:string}} x */ function temp1(x) { return x.text; }
/** @param {!Foo} x */ function temp2(x) { /** @notypecheck */ function g() { return temp1(x); } return g(); }
/** @param {{text:string}} x */ function temp3(x) {}
function callee() { temp2(new Foo()); temp3(new Foo()); }
```
`temp2(new Foo())` still drives `matchConstraint` on a non-native `Foo` instance, which infers `text` as `(string|undefined)`, so `temp3(new Foo())` can still get the same kind of wrong value out of the same computation.

SIBLING PATHS: No separate sibling caller path is needed, because the residue is still inside the same computation in `PrototypeObjectType.matchConstraint` for non-native named/prototyped objects. I do not see another unpatched method in the listed reachable region that independently reproduces this same wrong-value computation without going through that remaining branch.

DECISION: OVERFITTING.

