REPORTED FAULT:  
For the call to `temp3` in `testIssue700`, the type checker computes the actual argument type wrongly and emits: `JSC_TYPE_MISMATCH. actual parameter 1 of temp3 does not match formal parameter found   : {b: boolean, n: number, text: (string|undefined)} required: (undefined|{b: boolean, n: number, text: string})`.  
The wrong value is the computed property type `text: (string|undefined)` on the actual argument.

ROOT CAUSE:  
`matchConstraint` applies record-type constraints to object types that should not be structurally mutated at all.  
The comment says this should happen only for anonymous types, but the original code mutates any `PrototypeObjectType`, including named/native ones, so a shared object type can incorrectly gain inferred optional properties like `text: (string|undefined)`.

PATCH EFFECT:  
Inside the `constraintObj.isRecordType()` branch, the patch returns immediately when `isNativeObjectType()` is true.  
So record constraints are no longer matched onto native object types, preventing this specific test from mutating the native `Object` type.

PATCH SCOPE:  
The property that makes the computation wrong is “being a non-anonymous object type that must not be mutated by record matching.”  
The patch does not key on that property; it keys only on one subset, `isNativeObjectType()`, which matches the failing test’s path rather than the full condition described by the method comment.

SURVIVING INPUT:  
A concrete surviving input is a named, non-native instance type:
```js
/** @constructor */ function Foo() {}
/** @param {{text:string}} x */ function temp1(x) { return x.text; }
/** @param {Foo} foo */ function trigger(foo) {
  /** @notypecheck */ function inner() { return temp1(foo); }
  return inner();
}
/** @param {{text:string}} x */ function temp3(x) {}
function callee() {
  var f = new Foo();
  f.text = 'a';
  temp3(f);
}
```
`trigger` can still cause `matchConstraint` to infer `Foo.text` as `(string|undefined)`, and then `temp3(f)` can get the same kind of wrong value in the mismatch: found `Foo` with `text: (string|undefined)` instead of `text: string`.

SIBLING PATHS:  
No separate sibling function in the listed reachable region appears to carry an independent copy of this computation; the residue is in `matchConstraint` itself.  
The surviving bad behavior comes from another caller path that still reaches the unpatched part of the same method on non-native named object types.

DECISION:  
OVERFITTING.

